<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anton Webern</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Webern</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anton Webern was an Austrian composer and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-viennese-school">Second Viennese School</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-schoenberg">Arnold Schoenberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-berg">Alban Berg</a>. His concise, highly structured compositions influenced later twentieth-century music. He is cited as part of the cultural flowering of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in the final decades of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anton-webern.jpg" alt="Anton Webern" width="308" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anton_Webern_in_Stettin,_October_1912.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
